"Model: a real convection calculation, in 2D. A slab of mantle as deep as the planet's mantle and half its circumference wide, its base held at the core's temperature and its top at the surface's, heated also from inside by radioactive decay (H), its sides insulating. The rock is a very stiff fluid (no inertia, the Boussinesq approximation); each step solves for the flow exactly from the temperatures, then carries and conducts the heat. Cold rock is stiffer by up to {#}⁵ (the linearized Arrhenius law), and the lid breaks where the stress passes its strength (a yield stress, Moresi and Solomatov {#}). Simplified: 2D, not a sphere (the bent picture wraps the flat slab round the core, so its inner edge is drawn shorter than in the model); {#} cells deep (about {#} km each for Earth), so the thinnest hot and cold layers are only two or three cells thick; Rayleigh numbers of at most {#}⁷, while Earth's mantle is about {#}⁷ to {#}⁹, so the model's plates move a few times slower than Earth's ({#} to {#} cm/yr). The constant viscosity flow matches the community benchmark (Blankenbach et al. {#}) to {#}% in heat flow; with a viscosity that changes a thousandfold with temperature (the benchmark's case 2a) this grid gives about {#}% too much heat flow, so the numbers are a guide to the regimes, not exact values. Times and speeds use the mantle's depth (Earth {#}, Venus {#}, Mars {#} km) and a thermal diffusivity of {#}⁻⁶ m²/s (Turcotte and Schubert {#}).",
